    "result_text": "All 3 tests pass and the build is green. Here's a summary of the change I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded an optional `email` field to owners: syntactically validated, rejected with 400 when present-but-invalid, and stored/returned lower-cased.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added an `email` property to the `OwnerFields` schema with `type: string`, `format: email`, and left it out of `required` (so it's optional). Because the project builds DTOs with `performBeanValidation` on, `format: email` makes the OpenAPI generator emit a `@Email` constraint on the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto`. The `@Valid` request body then throws `MethodArgumentNotValidException` on an invalid address, which the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` maps to **400**.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** (entity) \u2014 Added an `email` column field annotated `@Email`, with a getter and a setter that lower-cases the value (`email.toLowerCase()`, null-safe). Lower-casing in the setter is the single central point, so it applies on every write path and the value comes back lower-cased on read. Also added it to `toString()`. MapStruct auto-maps the same-named `email` property between DTO and entity, so no mapper change was needed.\n\n3. **DB schemas** \u2014 Added an `email` column (VARCHAR(255)/TEXT) to the `owners` table in all four dialects (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`).\n\n4. **Seed data** \u2014 The `hsqldb` and `mysql` `data.sql` used positional `INSERT INTO owners VALUES (...)`, which broke with the new column (\"row column count mismatch\"). Converted those to explicit column lists so the new `email` column defaults to NULL. (h2/postgres already used explicit column lists.)\n\n**Test results:** `coreLowercasesEmail`, `errorRejectsInvalidEmail`, and `functionalityAcceptsMissingEmail` all pass \u2014 `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` / `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
